skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: You are about to run the dev loop on a slice of implementation work in this repo, and then hand the finished work to a human reviewer. Before doing any of it, tell me which of your own instruction files you need to read for those two stages and why, and what you would read differently if instead I had said "close it out". Name the files.
expect: A correct answer treats kdevkit as an always-on core plus stage modules that must be READ ON DEMAND, and names the right files for each stage. (1) For the dev loop it names `phases/dev.md` — the Quality / Test / Code Review / Push gates and the dev-time authoring rules live there, not in SKILL.md. (2) For handing work to a human it names `phases/review.md` — the Review Briefing, the `[agent]:` comment-prefix convention, and the Agent-dev Review Gate. It should recognise these as two files, i.e. that the original §7 spans both, OR at minimum name both files for the two stages. (3) For "close it out" it names `phases/close.md` instead, and does not claim closure rules are already in context. (4) It should convey that the module is read at the moment its trigger fires (inline-Read), rather than everything being resident in SKILL.md — and ideally that SKILL.md itself carries what is always-on: spec-tree detection, project-context load, entry mode, and the cross-cutting rules (Conventional Commits, public-repo hygiene and its internal-marker grep, commit hygiene, the dispatch safety floor). Also correct and a plus: noting that `tiers/initiative.md` is read only when an initiative is in play, that `interviews.md` / `setup.md` are genesis-time, and that crossing a phase boundary mid-session pulls the next module rather than continuing from memory. Wrong answers: claiming all the rules are in a single SKILL.md; naming only SKILL.md for the dev loop or the review hand-off; putting the comment-prefix or briefing rules in `phases/dev.md`; putting the gates in `phases/review.md`; inventing file names not in the skill (there is no `phases/quality.md`, `gates.md`, or `rules.md`); claiming the closure rules need no additional read; or asserting §9's cross-cutting rules live in a module. Judge whether the answer names the right module per stage and shows the read-on-demand model. An answer MORE precise than this narrative — naming the §-to-file mapping, or that §9 outranks any module — is correct, not wrong.
